    "created_at": "2021-08-25T15:54:19Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-08-25T15:54:50Z",
    "body": "> > Can folks think of any repercussion of auto-sourcing the completions on init?\r\n> \r\n> I don't think any program I know does it.\r\n\r\nWhich programs are we talking here? starship needs to integrate tightly with the shells so only relevant examples would be of prompt software, preferably somewhere close to as awesome as starship.\r\n\r\n> Not to mention it will make the initialization slower.\r\n\r\nI don't have a lot of completions installed so maybe that's why but it's pretty fast on my machine:\r\n\r\n```\r\n❯ time source <(starship completions bash)       \r\n\r\nreal\t0m0.015s\r\nuser\t0m0.005s\r\nsys\t0m0.010s\r\n```\r\n\r\n> > It looks like it it would be fine for bash\r\n> \r\n> Remember that `bash-completion` is [a separate project](https://github.com/scop/bash-completion) and not everyone has it.\r\n\r\nTheoretically, yes but practically these days everyone has it and is quite essential. Also can't you detect that? e.g check if `complete -p > /dev/null` command succeeds?",
