    "created_at": "2021-08-25T17:11:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-08-25T17:11:21Z",
    "body": "> Which programs are we talking here? starship needs to integrate tightly with the shells so only relevant examples would be of prompt software\r\n\r\nAs simple examples, I have `fzf` and `zoxide` installed on this system. Neither of them have completions installed by default. FZF *does* have completions, but they're added by [sourcing an additional script](https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/fzf#Configuration).\r\n\r\n> Also can't you detect that? e.g check if `complete -p > /dev/null` command succeeds\r\n\r\nIf possible, this would be something to potentially discuss. On [this issue](https://github.com/junegunn/fzf/issues/2392) on the fzf repo, junegunn seems to claim that this can't always be reliably done for bash, but I'm not entirely sure why.\r\n\r\nEvery other project that I've seen separates completions from other functionality, and without knowing why, I'd be nervous about changing that for our project. This goes double given that the alternative is adding a single line in `.bashrc`. If it really is just perfomance, then I wouldn't feel as bad about it, but I'd hate to throw it in, learn that there's some really nasty corner case with conflicting completions, and be forced to revert.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/starship/starship/issues/comments/905719438/reactions",
