    "updated_at": "2021-08-27T03:33:30Z",
    "body": "> starship needs to integrate tightly with the shells\n\nYes and no, Starship will never be able to integrate with the shell as tight as plugins written in shell because it's stateless and shell agnostic. Though the integration can still be improved.\n\n> so only relevant examples would be of prompt software, preferably somewhere close to as awesome as starship\n\nSome other cross shell projects I know including https://github.com/JanDeDobbeleer/oh-my-posh and https://github.com/justjanne/powerline-go don't have a command line interface to interact with, so they are excluded. That said, I don't think we need to limit it to prompts and @chipbuster provides some modern CLI programs as examples which won't install completions during initialization.\n\n> Theoretically, yes but practically these days everyone has it and is quite essential. Also can't you detect that? e.g check if complete -p > /dev/null command succeeds?\n\nYou made a point but it's not trivial. ",
