    "created_at": "2021-12-23T22:30:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-12-23T22:30:12Z",
    "body": "> Remember that bash-completion is a separate project and not everyone has it.\r\n\r\n@kidonng what do you mean by separate project? Both `complete` and `compgen` appear to be builtin functions in Bash.\r\n\r\n> Every other project that I've seen separates completions from other functionality, and without knowing why, I'd be nervous about changing that for our project.\r\n\r\n@chipbuster I would think that the primary reason is performance. Imagine if every binary you installed required you to add another line `eval \"$(foo completion bash)\"` to your `.bashrc` -- every `eval` statement would have to launch an external process, and your shell would get slow pretty soon.\r\n\r\n`starship` and `zoxide` are uniquely positioned in the sense that they anyway require users to call `eval` once in order to be initialized. Since that cost is already being paid, it may be worth looking into whether running a few hundred lines of completions would have any measurable impact on startup time.",
